    "body": "EICMA 2026 • MTPeak Stole Some of the Spotlight at EICMA 2026 With Insane Power and Retro Looks on Three Wheels\n2027 Ford Ranger Wolftrak Special Edition Comes to Europe With Exclusive Perks and Diesel or PHEV\n8 photos\nPhoto: Ford\nThe US automaker is taking care of the Ford Ranger on the Old Continent with a new special edition dubbed Wolftrak that is arriving in early 2027 for both diesel and plug-in hybrid models.\nThe Ford Motor Company has been pretty busy lately: they just got into the spacecraft manufacturing business after recently becoming a battery cell manufacturer by purchasing a stake in Revolution Space. They are also targeting Japanese customers with the Ford Ranger, F-150, and Super Duty pickup trucks.\nOver in Australia, their workhorse Ford Ranger Super Duty just got a railway pass with the Hy-Rail conversion, and on the Old Continent the latest Blue Oval news is also about the Ford Ranger – the regular one, not the Super Duty . More precisely, the local subsidiary of the US automaker is launching a new special edition called Ford Ranger Wolftrak that is ready for both “work and play.”\nAccording to the company and Samir Jusupovic, who is Ranger’s brand manager in Europe , the new limited series 2027 Ford Ranger Wolftrak “pairs bold, off-road-inspired styling and a more refined cabin with the real-world capability expected of an International Pick-Up Award winner and Europe’s best-selling pickup for the past 11 consecutive years.”\nDeveloped starting from the Ford Ranger Wildtrak , this limited edition model comes with both practical and exclusive perks, targeting users who go to “tough work sites” but also use the same vehicle “in their downtime.” Ford says that a Super Duty would be too much and a Raptor might not have the full range of capabilities, so the Ranger Wolftrak tries to hit the sweet spot, with a choice of the 3.0-liter V6 turbodiesel or the plug-in hybrid (PHEV) setup.\nThe highlights include a one-ton payload and 3.5-tonne braked towing capability, the availability of both the four-door and five-seat Double Cab (V6 turbodiesel or PHEV) as well as the two-door four-seat Super Cab (V6 turbodiesel), and the model also comes with exclusive perks. Those range from the bespoke Traction Green exterior color to Wolftrak-only black touches, and from the black 18-inch alloy wheels with all-terrain tires to the long-leg sports bar and the “easy-clean” Ebony interior with Traction Green touches and Wolftrak-embroidered seats.\nThere’s also a digital instrument cluster, SYNC 4 on a 12-inch vertical touchscreen, full-time intelligent all-wheel drive, Pro Trailer Backup Assist, a 360-degree camera, plus Blind Spot Monitor with Trailering among other things. Customers who choose the PHEV powertrain also get the optional Pro Power Onboard, which outputs up to 6.9 kW through two load bed sockets.\nThe Ford Ranger turbodiesel has 237 hp and 600 Nm or 443 lb-ft of torque, and the PHEV model has a combined 275 hp and 690 or 509 lb-ft of torque, along with up to 27 miles of all-electric range. The new Wolftrak special edition will become available to order before the end of the year, with first deliveries in late spring of 2027.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2027 Ford Ranger Wolftrak Ford Ranger Ford Ranger Wolftrak turbodiesel PHEV Europe\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT Go Mango 1970 Dodge Super Bee Has Seen Better Days, Needs Second Chance",
